          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"The rose\",\"addressee\":\"The little prince\",\"anchor_quote\":\"je t'aime\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The rose directly avows a present loving state and explains that the little prince failed to recognize it because of her own conduct. This truth-presenting confession is the expression's dominant force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The avowal does not create, renew, or invoke an obligation or undertaking by the rose. In the same turn she accepts the separation and releases the little prince rather than using the expression to bind herself or him. Her wish that he be happy and her instructions about leaving are separate speech acts, not promises carried by “je t'aime.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu seras loin, toi.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The expression is a deliberate explanatory confession, not a reflex-like affective discharge. The scene explicitly characterizes the rose's manner as calm, despite the sadness surrounding the farewell.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's core function is adequately represented by T as an avowal of love. Its corrective and revelatory role—telling him what he had not understood—does not constitute a distinct core mode beyond that avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"During the little prince's departure, after apologizing for her foolishness, the rose calmly reveals that she loves him and acknowledges that her behavior prevented him from knowing it. She nevertheless accepts his departure, urges him to be happy, and conceals her own tears.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No relational undertaking is made or renewed by the focal expression. The rose avows previously concealed affection while accepting that the little prince will leave. Her directives that he be happy, leave the globe alone, and go are distinct acts. The evidence therefore presents no commitment requiring uptake and no acceptance or rejection of such a commitment.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince, specifically the rose's farewell to the little prince.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports the represented sincerity of the rose's affection through her apology, concealed crying, and the earlier narration about her tenderness. Nevertheless, literary narration cannot independently verify an inner state beyond how the scene represents it. No omitted context is assumed.\"}"
        }
      ],
